WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.380
Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:05.650
Tesla CEO Elon Musk is not a fan of LIDAR
- the laser sensor used by many autonomous

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.970
car companies to navigate the world.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:13.410
LIDAR is a fool’s errand and anyone relying
on LIDAR is doomed.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.100
LIDAR stands for Light Detection and Ranging.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.789
The sensor emits pulsed light waves into the
environment.

00:00:18.789 --> 00:00:22.439
They bounce off surrounding objects and return
back to the sensor.

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:26.359
The sensor uses the time it took for each
pulse to return to the sensor to calculate

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:28.329
the distance it traveled.

00:00:28.329 --> 00:00:32.640
Repeating this a million times a second helps
create a 3D map of the environment.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.680
If the LIDAR detects it’s getting close
to an object, it’ll apply the brakes to

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.360
slow down the vehicle.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:42.650
Practically everyone other than Tesla views
LIDAR as essential.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:46.870
Alphabet's Waymo – seen as one of the frontrunners
of self-driving - uses LIDAR.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:51.590
GM’s self-driving unit Cruise acquired its
own LIDAR manufacturer a few years ago.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.510
Apple’s rumored smart car is said to be
looking for the sensor.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.470
One reason Musk doesn’t like LIDAR is that
it’s expensive.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.400
It’s like having a whole bunch of expensive
appendices.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.940
One appendix is bad. Well, now we're going to put a whole bunch of them.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.828
That’s ridiculous.

00:01:05.828 --> 00:01:06.671
You’ll see.

00:01:06.671 --> 00:01:10.890
LIDAR used to be very expensive, costing tens
of thousands of dollars.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:14.740
But now, LIDAR units can be thousands of dollars
or under $1,000.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:19.469
And manufacturers aspire to reduce costs to
the low hundreds in the future.

00:01:19.469 --> 00:01:22.890
Supporters of LIDAR argue it can do things
cameras cannot.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.890
While cameras interpret depth, LIDAR precisely
measures distances.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:31.439
It’s trusted by NASA to do everything from
studying changes to glaciers to collecting

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:33.649
data over the Amazon rainforest.

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:38.810
However, Musk argues that LIDAR cannot fully
capture the quirks of the real world.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.340
He’s called it a crutch.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.100
Whereas Tesla’s approach to self-driving
is to rely on cameras in the way humans depend

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.869
on vision to drive.

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:51.110
To use just computer vision, it would have
to understand an image in the way we understand

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.950
an image with our eyes and brain.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.770
Musk thinks it can be done.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.689
Which begs the question: Why was Tesla spotted
with LIDAR?

00:01:58.689 --> 00:02:03.619
An autonomous vehicle consultant recently
posted these photos on Twitter of a Model

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:06.249
Y that was sporting LIDAR in Florida.

00:02:06.249 --> 00:02:11.050
According to a report by Automotive News,
Tesla has a contract with LIDAR manufacturer

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.380
Luminar Technologies though neither Luminar
nor Tesla has confirmed it.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.420
I don’t think that they’ll ever actually use LIDAR on their production vehicles.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:24.530
I spoke with Sam Abuelsamid, an analyst with
Guidehouse Insights, who believes Tesla is

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.550
simply using LIDAR for training purposes.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:32.220
If your machine vision system is saying there’s
a vehicle 50  m away from you in the lane to

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.910
your right, you want to have something to
use to verify that the software is calculating

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:39.490
that correctly, and that’s what LIDAR does very well.

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:43.770
He says if Tesla were to change its mind and
incorporate LIDAR, that would be problematic

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.610
for its fleet of a million existing vehicles.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:51.200
Musk assured customers that their Teslas already
had all the hardware necessary for full self-driving.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.720
In the same way Tesla feels it doesn’t need
LIDAR, it’s also doing away with radar.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.280
Tesla’s website used to refer to radar for
its driver assistance system, Autopilot.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.240
However, the references to radar have recently
been dropped.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:09.940
The company confirmed Model 3s and Model Ys
delivered to the North American market are

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:12.020
no longer equipped with radar.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.580
Model S and Model Xs will eventually make
the transition too as will cars destined for

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.000
other markets.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.490
Tesla says new vehicles without radar will
have Autopilot temporarily limited to lower

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:27.720
speeds and a longer minimum following distance
and will lose a few other functions.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.610
Removing radar isn’t surprising.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:35.800
Musk tweeted in 2018 that “Long-term, the
car will work purely on vision”.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:40.620
The company is going all in on what it calls
“Tesla Vision” where Autopilot and full

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.680
self-driving - its most advanced driver assistance
system - will rely purely on camera vision

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.960
and neural net processing.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:53.010
This is what Tesla Autopilot sees using neural
networks that analyze the vehicle’s surroundings.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:57.820
The aim is for Tesla’s artificial intelligence
technologies to propel its system from Level

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:02.220
2 autonomy all the way to Level 5 in which
a driver would become a passenger.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.040
There would be no steering wheel or brakes.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.790
Tesla believes that is possible without radar
or LIDAR though some analysts question whether

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.270
that’s the best move.

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:16.031
Although Algolux is a company that focuses
on using cameras for autonomous driving, an

00:04:16.031 --> 00:04:20.530
executive there tells me relying solely on
cameras may not be the best option:

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:24.780
Dave Tokic says “…it may be short-sighted
to not leverage additional sensor modalities,

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.000
especially new breakthroughs in radar…as
well as Lidar if the market can absorb it.”

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.630
Despite his adamant belief that LIDAR will
be unnecessary, Musk once admitted: Perhaps

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.060
I am wrong, and I will look like a fool.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.500
But I am quite certain that I am not.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:43.380
Tesla is relying heavily on artificial intelligence
as it prepares for a driverless future.

